<onlyinclude>'''Photolithography''' is a way of drawing extremely small patterns on a chip wafer using light and a light-sensitive coating called ''photoresist''. The pattern tells later manufacturing steps where to remove, add or change material.</onlyinclude>

==How it works==
Imagine projecting a stencil onto a surface covered in special paint. A lithography machine projects a carefully controlled pattern onto photoresist. After exposure and chemical development, some resist remains while other regions are cleared. The remaining pattern protects or exposes selected parts of the wafer during a later manufacturing step.

The main stages are coating, aligning, exposing, developing and checking the pattern. Engineers repeat lithography at different stages of chip production; one exposure does not create an entire processor.

==Why smaller patterns are difficult==
Shorter-wavelength light and better optics can resolve finer details. But tiny errors in focus, alignment or resist response can spoil a pattern. Modern manufacturing uses both deep-ultraviolet and extreme-ultraviolet light, depending on the layer and process.<ref>[https://www.asml.com/en/technology/lithography-principles/rayleigh-criterion ASML, Rayleigh criterion].</ref>

==Key distinction==
Lithography defines a pattern; subsequent processes such as etching or deposition transfer or use it. A printed feature is not automatically a functioning transistor.

